<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Keats</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Keats was an English poet of the second generation of Romantic poets, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-bysshe-shelley">Percy Bysshe Shelley</a>, born in Moorgate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His poems had been in publication for less than four years when he died of tuberculosis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> at the age of 25, and they were indifferently received in his lifetime; Blackwood's Magazine attacked Endymion. His fame grew rapidly after his death, and by the end of the century he was placed in the canon of English literature. His odes, among them &quot;Ode to a Nightingale&quot; and &quot;Ode on a Grecian Urn&quot;, and medievalist poems such as &quot;The Eve of St. Agnes&quot; influenced the Pre-Raphaelite movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-keats.jpg" alt="John Keats" width="330" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Hilton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Keats_by_William_Hilton_circa_1822_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Keats was born in Moorgate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 31 October 1795, to Thomas and Frances Keats, and was the eldest of four surviving children. Keats believed he was born at the Swan and Hoop Inn, which his grandfather owned, but there is no evidence for this. In 1803 John was sent to John Clarke's school in Enfield, whose liberal outlook and modern curriculum nurtured his interest in classics and history. In October 1815 he registered as a medical student at Guy's Hospital and was soon made a dresser, assisting surgeons during operations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Keats devoted more and more time to literature. In May 1816 Leigh Hunt published his sonnet &quot;O Solitude&quot; in The Examiner, his first poem in print, and in October Charles Cowden Clarke introduced him to Hunt. His first volume, Poems (1817), was a critical failure; his publishers felt ashamed of it and he moved to Taylor and Hessey, who planned a new volume within a month.</p><p>He moved to Wentworth Place, Hampstead, where the winter of 1818 to 1819 began his annus mirabilis; he composed five of his six great odes there in April and May 1819. According to Brown, &quot;Ode to a Nightingale&quot; was written under a plum tree in the garden. In his lifetime his three volumes probably sold only about 200 copies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 3 February 1820 Keats coughed up blood and said to Brown: &quot;That drop of blood is my death warrant. I must die.&quot; On the advice of his doctors he sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> with Joseph Severn on the Maria Crowther in September, was held in quarantine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> for ten days and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 14 November. He died there on 23 February 1821 and was buried in the Protestant Cemetery under a stone bearing, at his request, no name but the words &quot;Here lies One whose Name was writ in Water&quot;. Severn and Brown added lines of protest at the critical reception of his work, and Hunt blamed the Quarterly Review's attack on Endymion for his death.</p><p>None of his biographers knew him. Shelley promoted the image of a poet too fine-tuned to endure life, which became the image of the suffering consumptive, and the first full biography, by Richard Monckton Milnes, appeared in 1848. His letters, first published in 1848 and 1878, are now almost as admired as his poetry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-s-eliot">T. S. Eliot</a> called them &quot;certainly the most notable and most important ever written by any English poet&quot;.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Keats” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The second generation of British poets died young: <b>John Keats</b> in Rome in 1821, Shelley drowned off the Italian coast in 1822 and Byron of fever at Missolonghi in 1824, while supporting the Greeks.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>George Chapman's translation inspired <b>John Keats</b>'s sonnet of 1816, and James Joyce's Ulysses of 1922 reworked the poem in the Dublin of his own day; Joyce's use of the myth in turn inspired Joseph Campbell's idea of the hero's journey, which most scholars today consider reductive.</blockquote><a href="/work/odyssey">Odyssey · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When old age shall this generation waste, Thou shalt remain, in midst of other woe Than ours, a friend to man, to whom thou say'st, 'Beauty is truth, truth beauty,'—that is all Ye know on earth, and all ye need to know.&#8221;</p><footer>&quot;Ode on a Grecian Urn&quot; (1820) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_complete_poetical_works_and_letters_of_John_Keats/Ode_on_a_Grecian_Urn" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I mean Negative Capability, that is when man is capable of being in uncertainties, Mysteries, doubts, without any irritable reaching after fact &amp; reason…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his brothers George and Thomas, December 1817 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Letter_to_George_and_Thomas_Keats,_December_28,_1817" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
